# USB-to-UART Converter PCB Design Report

## Design Phase Report (KiCad 9)

**Project Title:** USB-to-UART Converter PCB Design

**Software Used:** KiCad 9 PCB Design Suite

**Project Type:** Two-Layer PCB Design

---

# 1. Project Overview

The objective of this project was to design a compact and manufacturable two-layer Printed Circuit Board (PCB) for a USB-to-UART converter using KiCad 9. The board enables communication between a host computer via USB and external embedded systems through a UART interface.

The work presented in this report covers only the PCB design stage, beginning from schematic capture and ending with a completed routed PCB layout.

---

# 2. Design Objectives

The primary objectives of the design were:

* Develop a complete circuit schematic.
* Select appropriate electronic components and footprints.
* Design a compact two-layer PCB.
* Ensure logical component placement.
* Route all electrical connections.
* Implement proper grounding.
* Prepare the design for further verification and manufacturing.

---

# 3. Design Environment

| Parameter          | Description                 |
| ------------------ | --------------------------- |
| EDA Software       | KiCad 9                     |
| PCB Type           | Two Layer                   |
| Design Methodology | Schematic Driven PCB Design |
| Board Thickness    | 1.6 mm                      |

---

# 4. Design Workflow

The PCB design followed a standard engineering workflow.

```
Requirement Analysis
        ↓
Circuit Design
        ↓
Schematic Capture
        ↓
Symbol Assignment
        ↓
Footprint Assignment
        ↓
Net Generation
        ↓
PCB Layout
        ↓
Component Placement
        ↓
Routing
        ↓
Ground Plane
        ↓
Final PCB Layout
```

---

# 5. Schematic Design

The schematic was developed in KiCad Schematic Editor.

Major design activities included:

* Component selection
* Symbol placement
* Power network creation
* USB interface connections
* UART interface connections
* Passive component integration
* Connector integration
* Net labeling

The schematic serves as the electrical blueprint for the PCB layout.

---

# 6. PCB Layout Design

The PCB layout was created using KiCad PCB Editor.

Major design tasks included:

* Importing schematic netlist
* Board outline definition
* Component placement
* Interactive routing
* Via placement
* Copper zone generation
* Silkscreen preparation

---

# 7. Component Placement Strategy

The placement strategy was developed to reduce routing complexity and maintain signal flow.

Key considerations included:

* USB connector positioned at the board edge.
* USB-to-UART IC placed centrally.
* Decoupling capacitors located close to power pins.
* UART header positioned for easy external access.
* Passive components grouped around the controller IC.
* Short routing paths between interconnected devices.

---

# 8. Routing Strategy

Manual routing techniques were adopted.

Routing objectives included:

* Minimize unnecessary trace length.
* Reduce routing congestion.
* Maintain clean signal paths.
* Minimize via usage.
* Organize top and bottom layer routing efficiently.

---

# 9. Grounding Strategy

Grounding was implemented using copper zones.

The objectives were:

* Improve return current paths.
* Reduce ground impedance.
* Enhance PCB stability.
* Simplify routing.

---

# 10. PCB Features

The completed PCB incorporates:

* USB interface connector
* UART communication header
* Power distribution network
* Ground plane implementation
* Silkscreen component identification
* Two-layer routing architecture

---

# 11. PCB Layout Summary

The PCB design includes:

* Complete component placement
* Fully routed signal traces
* Ground copper zones
* Silkscreen annotations
* Board outline definition

The layout is organized to support ease of assembly and readability.

---

# 12. Design Considerations

The following PCB design practices were considered:

* Logical component grouping
* Short signal routing
* Efficient use of PCB area
* Clear connector accessibility
* Consistent component orientation
* Compact board dimensions

---

